firefox: carry the scroll bar pair to web pages

A write pass now writes a second owned sheet,
qtcurve-userContent.css, beside the chrome sheet of each profile,
and it merges one import line into userContent.css. The sheet
holds one universal rule with the scroll bar pair of the chrome
sheet under !important: a scroller with a page rule takes no
inherited user value, and an ordinary user rule loses to a page
rule. Pages that state their own scroll bar colours now follow
the QtCurve pair.

The sheet carries no scrollbar-width row. The Firefox width
values are three words, no QtCurve option maps to them, and a
constant row would beat the thin choice of a page for no reason.

The same settings key and the same add-on state govern both
sheets. A write pass writes both sheets or reports the first
fault, and a remove pass takes both import lines and both owned
sheets away; a fault on one sheet pair does not stop the other
pair. The one stylesheets preference covers both sheets, so the
notice of about:config does not change. The watch mode watches
the userContent.css file of each profile beside the chrome sheet.

The mode --stdout-content prints the content sheet. mergeImport
and removeImport take the import statement as a parameter, so one
body serves the two sheets of this writer and the one sheet of
the GTK writer. assert-ff-content.sh proves the rule shape, the
pair and the absent width row, and the profile checks prove the
two-file flows.

The change sits on the branch work/firefox-theme of the local tree, as
commit 12 of 13. The branch holds those thirteen commits above the head of
work/gtk4-theme, which merge request !15 on system/qtcurve holds. The fork
wyn/qtcurve holds work/firefox-theme. Merge request !29 on system/qtcurve
holds that branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

